// TEST: Bare Comma Tuple Construction
// The CONSTRUCTION half of plan issue #10 (docs/plan-tuples-vs-arg-packs.md
// 6b, Design C): a bare comma on the right of a `let` builds a tuple, at any
// width >= 2. Design C makes this load-bearing -- `Tuple<N>` annotations are
// only useful if there is an unparenthesized way to write the value.
//
// The binder side (`let a, b = t`) stays DEFERRED; only the RHS changed.
// Parser.parseLetRhs is shared by all three let sites, so the top-level form
// here and the block form below cannot drift.

let b = 1.0
let c = 2.0

// Width 2, top-level. `t` itself is Unit-invisible to the auto-printer (a
// tuple binding prints nothing), so the pin is on the projections -- which
// is also what makes this a ROUND TRIP: construct with a comma, read back
// with `t[k]`.
let t = b, c
let t0 = t[0]
let t1 = t[1]
// EXPECT: t0 = 1
// EXPECT: t1 = 2

// Width 3. Nothing about the rule is 2-specific.
let a3 = 10.0, 20.0, 30.0
let s3 = a3[0] + a3[1] + a3[2]
// EXPECT: s3 = 60

// PRECEDENCE. Each component parses at full expression precedence and stops
// at the comma, so this is a 2-tuple of two CALLS, not the single call
// `dbl(3.0, dbl(4.0))`. If the comma bound tighter than application the two
// readings would differ by an arity error, so this pins the split.
function dbl(v: Float64) -> Float64 = v * 2.0
let pc = dbl(3.0), dbl(4.0)
let pcv = pc[0] + pc[1]
// EXPECT: pcv = 14

// Operators inside a component, for the same reason: `+` binds tighter than
// the comma, so this is (3, 30), not 3 + (2, 30).
let po = b + 2.0, c * 15.0
let po0 = po[0]
let po1 = po[1]
// EXPECT: po0 = 3
// EXPECT: po1 = 30

// BLOCK let (Parser.parseLetStmt), the second of the three sites.
function inBlock(x: Float64) -> Float64 = {
    let p = x, x * 2.0
    p[0] + p[1]
}
let blockSum = inBlock(3.0)
// EXPECT: blockSum = 9

// The parenthesized literal is unchanged and produces the SAME node
// (ExprTuple), so the two spellings are one program from the checker down.
let paren = (4.0, 5.0)
let parenv = paren[0] + paren[1]
// EXPECT: parenv = 9
